# Inactive v2 trusted issuer implementation

New source only: `src/lib/ai/conversation/service/voice-v2-issuance.server.ts`.
SHA256: `39b9beb9b45fed11ddf863ee180f480fa1547a3777afca3715526f8cddd00f10`.

The root-approved `createVoiceV2AuthorityIssuer(ports)` API returns one-shot `issue`, synchronous `cancel` and a content-free `snapshot`. The exact input is an actual trusted `ConversationService` plus protected thread ID, revision and exact six-digit UTC source creation timestamp. Request-bound authentication returns exactly `{ownerId, sessionId, expiresAt}`; the issuer derives the v2 query pair rather than accepting caller identity or timing. Port functions and the explicit integer 1..900000 ms authority cap are captured once. The host must establish provenance; object shapes and `instanceof` do not do so.

Stages: validate/capture owned snapshot and lifecycle; record conservative issue/verification start; authenticate; read initial parent; reauthenticate; observe parent once; revalidate Auth/parent/current thread; register/observe generation once; revalidate; seal exact service snapshot with unchanged local P3b seal and HMAC; revalidate; register exact new immutable fingerprint and v2 parent association once; revalidate; read exact issuance; final Auth/parent/current-thread/Auth sequence and immediate local liveness check before the sole token return. A null first parent read only selects expected version zero; the authoritative observation still controls tombstones and legacy exclusion.

All waits retain original verification time and issue time, monotonic elapsed time strictly below 30000 ms, original owner/session, parent version, source/revision, prior nonnull incarnation, service lifecycle and exact snapshot. Eligibility can only shrink under configured cap, fresh Auth policy and current allowlist; an already-sealed envelope is discarded if a later shorter ceiling no longer covers it. The issuer never reseals to replace uncertainty. Protocol 2 is exact at all receipt boundaries; no v1 fallback or browser/raw-token backfill exists.

Each of three possible writes sets its attempted flag and retains a frozen detached exact request before invocation. Matching detached frozen acknowledgments are retained before late/cancelled liveness rejection. Snapshot includes only state, expected parent version, these three requests/acknowledgments and attempted flags; no service/exported authority, private writing or sealed token. Missing/malformed/lost replies remain unconfirmed; none can trigger retry or replacement local identity. Local cancellation cannot undo SQL, revoke remote Auth, retract a released token or prove provider termination.

